  "final_text": "Both objects exist, and the rule is enabled and running.\n\n**Data explored (`kibana_sample_data_logs`, 14,074 docs):**\n- `response.keyword` holds the status code. The values are 200 (12,832), 404 (801), and 503 (441). There are no other 5xx codes, so the 5xx filter is effectively 503s.\n- Other useful fields: `clientip` (unique visitors), `geo.dest` (top sources CN, IN, US), `timestamp` (the time field), `bytes`, and `url`.\n- The timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, which is partly in the future relative to today (2026-10-11).\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-f8243d90 Web logs overview`** (id `d4b8b3b8-98d7-4b82-b2c5-b6e2ed216850`)\n- Total requests (count)\n- 5xx responses (count, KQL `response.keyword: 5*`)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Requests over time by status code (stacked bar, date histogram on `timestamp`, broken down by `response.keyword`)\n- Time range is set to 2026-09-01 through 2027-01-01 so the future-dated sample data is visible. I chose that range rather than \"last 15 minutes\" for this reason.\n\n**Rule: `EFF-f8243d90 5xx spike`** (id `bc1af18e-f218-4b99-a01a-461e47527b5c`)\n- Type: Elasticsearch query (DSL), `wildcard` on `response.keyword: 5*`, index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`\n- Condition: count > 10 over a 5-minute window\n- Schedule: every 1 minute\n- Enabled: yes. After about 75 seconds the last execution status was `ok`.\n\n**Choices I made:**\n- I used the `timestamp` field, not `@timestamp`, because it is the data view's time field and it is where the sample data sits.\n- The rule has no actions, since no connector was specified. It will record alerts but won't notify anyone until you add one.\n- I did not verify that the rule has fired. With the data dated in the future, the current 5-minute window may contain no 5xx events. Checking that would take a look at the alert history.",
